DO $operation$
DECLARE r jsonb; v jsonb; updated_count integer;
BEGIN
 FOR r IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements($batch$[{"id":"00211659-6543-40ab-b969-10aa6b689589","question":"Describe the physiological production of cortisol and the management of a patient with suspected HPA-axis suppression undergoing major surgery.","before":{"model_answer":"The adrenal cortex is organized into three distinct histological zones (Glomerulosa, Fasciculata, and Reticularis) producing mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and sex steroids respectively. Cortisol, the primary glucocorticoid, is synthesized from cholesterol under the regulation of the Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal (HPA) axis via CRH and ACTH. Its secretion follows a marked circadian rhythm, with peak concentrations typically between 07:00 and 09:00 (approx. 400-600 nmol/L) and a nadir around midnight.\n\nMetabolically, cortisol promotes gluconeogenesis, inhibits peripheral glucose uptake, and increases protein catabolism and lipolysis. It has a permissive effect on catecholamines, maintaining vascular tone and myocardial contractility. In response to major physiological stress, such as major surgery or sepsis, the normal daily production of approximately 10-20 mg increases significantly, potentially reaching 200 mg/day.\n\nClinically, the primary concern for anaesthetists is HPA axis suppression due to exogenous steroid use. Clinical suppression is generally considered likely if a patient has taken the equivalent of >5mg prednisolone daily for more than four weeks in the preceding year. Such patients require perioperative supplementation (e.g., the usual dose plus hydrocortisone 100mg IV at induction, followed by 200mg over 24 hours by infusion or 50mg IV every 6 hours for major surgery) to prevent Addisonian crisis, characterized by refractory hypotension, hyponatraemia, and hyperkalaemia. Assessment of the axis can be performed via the Short ACTH Stimulation (Synacthen) test, looking for a peak cortisol >450-500 nmol/L.","high_yield_points":["Glucocorticoid/Mineralocorticoid/Androgen split (G-F-R)","Inhibition of Gluneogenesis/Protein synthesis","Circadian rhythm (peak 08:00, trough 00:00)","Secondary vs Tertiary suppression causes","200mg/day maximum stress production"],"pitfalls":["Inability to name the three anatomical zones and their respective hormones.","Overestimating the dose of steroids required for minor 'clean' surgery.","Failing to recognize that mineralocorticoid activity is usually preserved in secondary adrenal insufficiency."]},"after":{"model_answer":"The adrenal cortex is organized into three distinct histological zones (Glomerulosa, Fasciculata, and Reticularis) producing mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and sex steroids respectively. Cortisol, the primary glucocorticoid, is synthesized from cholesterol under the regulation of the Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal (HPA) axis via CRH and ACTH. Its secretion follows a marked circadian rhythm, with peak concentrations typically between 07:00 and 09:00 (approx. 400-600 nmol/L) and a nadir around midnight.\n\nMetabolically, cortisol promotes gluconeogenesis, inhibits peripheral glucose uptake, and increases protein catabolism and lipolysis. It has a permissive effect on catecholamines, maintaining vascular tone and myocardial contractility. In response to major physiological stress, such as major surgery or sepsis, the normal daily production of approximately 10-20 mg increases significantly, potentially reaching 200 mg/day.\n\nIn adults, at least 5 mg prednisolone daily or equivalent for a month or more can suppress the HPA axis. Review the drug, dose, route and duration. For adults on adrenosuppressive steroids, corrected UK guideline Table 2 specifies hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at induction and an immediate infusion of 200 mg over 24 hours for intermediate or major surgery, in addition to usual steroid therapy. After major surgery continue parenteral cover while nil by mouth. After uncomplicated intermediate or major surgery, use double the usual oral dose for 48 hours once oral treatment is possible, then return to baseline. Adjust the plan for delayed recovery or vomiting. Established adrenal insufficiency has a separate pathway. Source: https://doi.org/10.1111/anae.15210","high_yield_points":["Glucocorticoid/Mineralocorticoid/Androgen split (G-F-R)","Cortisol promotes gluconeogenesis and protein catabolism.","Circadian rhythm (peak 08:00, trough 00:00)","Secondary vs Tertiary suppression causes","Cortisol production varies with illness and surgical stress."],"pitfalls":["Inability to name the three anatomical zones and their respective hormones.","Overestimating the dose of steroids required for minor 'clean' surgery.","Failing to recognize that mineralocorticoid activity is usually preserved in secondary adrenal insufficiency."]}},{"id":"079470dc-039d-434c-b209-af508b433e64","question":"You are providing anaesthesia for a Category 2 Caesarean Section for failure to progress. Following delivery of the placenta, the obstetrician informs you the uterus is atonic and blood loss has reached 1500ml and is ongoing. How would you manage this situation?","before":{"model_answer":"This clinical scenario represents a Major Obstetric Haemorrhage (MOH), traditionally defined as blood loss exceeding 1500ml or any volume leading to physiological instability. In the obstetric population, common causes follow the '4 Ts' framework: Tone (atony - 80%), Tissue (retained products), Trauma (lacerations/rupture), and Thrombin (coagulopathy).\n\nManagement must be multidisciplinary and concurrent. Initially, I would call for senior assistance (obstetric, anaesthetic, and midwifery) and trigger the hospital's MOH protocol to ensure blood products and a scribe are available. Immediate Resuscitation follows an ABC approach: high-flow oxygen, wide-bore IV access (minimum two 14G/16G cannulae), and rapid crystalloid/warmed blood administration using a rapid infuser. \n\nSpecific management of atony begins with mechanical uterine massage ('rubbing up the fundus') and pharmacological intervention. First-line is a slow IV bolus of Oxytocin (5 units), followed by an infusion (40 units in 500ml). Second-line agents include Ergometrine (500mcg IM/IV cautiously), Carboprost (250mcg IM every 15 mins, max 8 doses), and Misoprostol (800mcg PR/SL). \n\nCrucially, Tranexamic acid (1g IV) should be administered early as per the WOMAN trial. If bleeding persists, I would escalate to surgical interventions (B-Lynch suture, uterine artery embolisation, or hysterectomy) while monitoring coagulation via Point-of-Care Testing (ROTEM/TEG) to guide the replacement of fibrinogen and platelets. The goal is to maintain Hb >70g/L, Fibrinogen >2g/L, and Platelets >75 x 10^9/L.","high_yield_points":["Identification of signs: tachycardia, hypotension, 'boggy' uterus.","Major Obstetric Haemorrhage (MOH) trigger: 1500ml or clinical instability.","Mechanical (Rubbing up), Pharmacological, and Surgical tiers of management.","Carbetocin vs. Oxytocin properties.","Point-of-Care Testing (ROTEM/TEG) to guide fibrinogen replacement."],"pitfalls":["Failure to call for help early/triggering MOH protocol.","Giving Ergometrine to a pre-eclamptic/hypertensive patient.","Underestimating blood loss due to concealment or amniotic fluid dilution."]},"after":{"model_answer":"Ongoing loss of 1500 mL with uterine atony after caesarean birth is major postpartum haemorrhage. Call the obstetric, anaesthetic, midwifery and transfusion teams and activate the local major haemorrhage protocol. Assess circulation and ongoing bleeding, obtain large-bore IV access, send blood including coagulation and fibrinogen, and provide warmed fluids and blood components guided by active loss and the clinical response.\n\nTreat the cause while resuscitating. Massage the uterus and assess for retained tissue, trauma and coagulopathy. Select uterotonics according to prophylaxis already given, contraindications and the obstetric protocol. Avoid ergometrine in severe hypertension and assess asthma before carboprost. If using an oxytocin infusion, prescribe its concentration, rate and duration under the local protocol. Give tranexamic acid promptly for PPH and escalate early to obstetric haemorrhage control if bleeding persists.\n\nSources: https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng235/chapter/Recommendations and https://www.rcog.org.uk/guidance/browse-all-guidance/green-top-guidelines/prevention-and-management-of-postpartum-haemorrhage-green-top-guideline-no-52/","high_yield_points":["Identification of signs: tachycardia, hypotension, 'boggy' uterus.","Activate the local major obstetric haemorrhage protocol according to ongoing blood loss, clinical condition and escalation criteria.","Mechanical (Rubbing up), Pharmacological, and Surgical tiers of management.","Carbetocin vs. Oxytocin properties.","Point-of-Care Testing (ROTEM/TEG) to guide fibrinogen replacement."],"pitfalls":["Failure to call for help early/triggering MOH protocol.","Giving Ergometrine to a pre-eclamptic/hypertensive patient.","Underestimating blood loss due to concealment or amniotic fluid dilution."]}},{"id":"19b6f63a-aa52-4c2a-8977-e4dfddd1769d","question":"Explain the mechanism of HPA axis suppression and the rationale for perioperative steroid supplementation.","before":{"model_answer":"The Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal (HPA) axis is the neuroendocrine system regulating the stress response. Endogenous cortisol production follows a circadian rhythm, peaking in the early morning. Exogenous administration of corticosteroids results in negative feedback at the hypothalamus (inhibiting CRH) and the anterior pituitary (inhibiting ACTH). \n\nChronic suppression leads to atrophy of the zona fasciculata and reticularis of the adrenal cortex. While there is individual variation, suppression is generally a concern in patients taking more than 5-10mg of prednisolone (or equivalent) for more than four weeks. These patients may be unable to mount an appropriate cortisol response to the surgical stressor, leading to an 'Addisonian' or adrenal crisis characterized by refractory hypotension, hyponatraemia, and hyperkalaemia.\n\nPerioperative management depends on the 'surgical stress' grade:\n1. **Minor stress** (e.g., inguinal hernia): Usual morning dose, no extra cover.\n2. **Moderate stress** (e.g., cholecystectomy): Usual dose + 50mg IV hydrocortisone at induction, then 25mg IV 8-hourly for 24 hours.\n3. **Major stress** (e.g., Whipple's): Usual dose + 100mg IV hydrocortisone at induction, then 200mg over 24 hours by continuous infusion (or 50mg IV 6-hourly if an infusion is not practical) for 24–48 hours.\nThe goal is to mimic the natural physiological rise of cortisol (100–150mg/day) seen during major trauma or sepsis.","high_yield_points":["HPA axis: Hypothalamus (CRH), Pituitary (ACTH), Adrenal (Cortisol).","Negative feedback loop inhibition.","Threshold: >5-10mg prednisolone for >4 weeks.","Risk of secondary adrenal insufficiency.","Requirement for 'steroid cover' in major stress."],"pitfalls":["Underestimating the time required for HPA suppression (only 3 weeks).","Forgetting that patients on inhaled or topical steroids can still be suppressed if doses are high."]},"after":{"model_answer":"The Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal (HPA) axis is the neuroendocrine system regulating the stress response. Endogenous cortisol production follows a circadian rhythm, peaking in the early morning. Exogenous administration of corticosteroids results in negative feedback at the hypothalamus (inhibiting CRH) and the anterior pituitary (inhibiting ACTH). \n\nIn adults, at least 5 mg prednisolone daily or equivalent for a month or more can suppress the HPA axis. Review the drug, dose, route and duration. Prolonged treatment suppresses ACTH and can impair cortisol production during surgery. Secondary adrenal insufficiency usually preserves aldosterone secretion.\n\nFor adults on adrenosuppressive steroids, corrected UK guideline Table 2 specifies hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at induction and an immediate infusion of 200 mg over 24 hours for intermediate or major surgery, in addition to usual steroid therapy. After major surgery continue parenteral cover while nil by mouth. After uncomplicated intermediate or major surgery, use double the usual oral dose for 48 hours once oral treatment is possible, then return to baseline. Adjust the plan for delayed recovery or vomiting. Established adrenal insufficiency has a separate pathway. Source: https://doi.org/10.1111/anae.15210","high_yield_points":["HPA axis: Hypothalamus (CRH), Pituitary (ACTH), Adrenal (Cortisol).","Negative feedback loop inhibition.","In adults, at least 5 mg prednisolone daily or equivalent for a month or more can suppress the HPA axis. Review the drug, dose, route and duration.","Risk of secondary adrenal insufficiency.","Requirement for 'steroid cover' in major stress."],"pitfalls":["Assuming the same regimen applies to every procedure and steroid-exposed patient.","Forgetting that patients on inhaled or topical steroids can still be suppressed if doses are high."]}},{"id":"d3105fd5-afb1-4a27-b9d7-ccacdd7759ab","question":"A 24-year-old patient is admitted to the ICU with a diagnosis of Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA). Can you define DKA, outline your initial management priorities, and discuss the specific goals of treatment?","before":{"model_answer":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) is an endocrine emergency characterized by the biochemical triad of hyperglycaemia (usually >11 mmol/L), ketonaemia (capillary ketones >3.0 mmol/L or ketonuria 2+), and a metabolic acidosis (venous pH <7.3 or bicarbonate <15 mmol/L). It results from relative or absolute insulin deficiency combined with an excess of counter-regulatory hormones (glucagon, cortisol, catecholamines), leading to uninhibited lipolysis and ketone body production (beta-hydroxybutyrate).\n\nClinical management prioritizes fluid resuscitation, typically starting with 1 litre of 0.9% Sodium Chloride over the first hour; however, caution is required in young patients to avoid cerebral oedema and in the elderly to avoid fluid overload. Fixed-rate intravenous insulin infusion (FRIII) is commenced at 0.1 units/kg/hour. A critical clinical pearl is the early replacement of potassium; despite a total body potassium deficit, plasma levels may initially appear normal or high due to the extracellular shift in acidosis. If serum K+ is between 3.5 and 5.5 mmol/L, 40 mmol/L should be added to each litre of fluid. \n\nVital monitoring includes hourly capillary blood glucose and ketones, with a target ketone reduction of 0.5 mmol/L/hr. Common pitfalls include the premature cessation of long-acting basal insulin and the development of hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis due to excessive 0.9% saline administration, which can mimic non-resolution of DKA. For adults following JBDS, biochemical resolution requires blood ketones below 0.6 mmol/L and venous pH above 7.3. The decision to transition to a subcutaneous insulin regimen also takes account of whether the patient can eat and drink. Follow the local diabetes pathway for safe IV-to-subcutaneous overlap. Source: https://abcd.care/sites/default/files/site_uploads/JBDS_Guidelines_Current/JBDS_02_Single_page_pathway_March_2023.pdf","high_yield_points":["Biochemical triad: Hyperglycaemia, Ketonemia, Metabolic Acidosis.","Fluid resuscitation is the priority (1L/1hr initially).","Individualise potassium replacement using serial levels, renal function, urine output and the adult JBDS pathway.","Fixed-rate insulin infusion at 0.1 units/kg/hour.","Continue long-acting basal insulin (Lantus/Levemir).","Adult JBDS biochemical resolution requires blood ketones <0.6 mmol/L and venous pH >7.3. Bicarbonate and oral intake are not additional biochemical criteria."],"pitfalls":["Stopping long-acting (basal) insulin during FRIII.","Failure to recognize euglycaemic DKA (associated with SGLT-2 inhibitors).","Inadequate potassium replacement leading to life-threatening arrhythmias."]},"after":{"model_answer":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) is an endocrine emergency characterized by the biochemical triad of hyperglycaemia (usually >11 mmol/L), ketonaemia (capillary ketones >3.0 mmol/L or ketonuria 2+), and a metabolic acidosis (venous pH <7.3 or bicarbonate <15 mmol/L). It results from relative or absolute insulin deficiency combined with an excess of counter-regulatory hormones (glucagon, cortisol, catecholamines), leading to uninhibited lipolysis and ketone body production (beta-hydroxybutyrate).\n\nBegin appropriate IV fluids, assessing cardiac and renal limitations and reassessing circulation. Follow the adult JBDS fixed-rate insulin pathway. Check serum potassium, renal function and urine output before prescribing potassium. Under JBDS, when potassium is 3.5–5.5 mmol/L add 40 mmol potassium chloride per litre of replacement fluid if the patient is passing urine. Above 5.5 mmol/L omit potassium initially. Below 3.5 mmol/L obtain urgent senior review for additional replacement and the insulin plan. The 2024 international consensus advises delaying insulin until potassium exceeds 3.5 mmol/L in this situation. Anuria or dialysis requires specialist adjustment. Recheck potassium frequently.\n\nVital monitoring includes hourly capillary blood glucose and ketones, with a target ketone reduction of 0.5 mmol/L/hr. Common pitfalls include the premature cessation of long-acting basal insulin and the development of hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis due to excessive 0.9% saline administration, which can mimic non-resolution of DKA. For adults following JBDS, biochemical resolution requires blood ketones below 0.6 mmol/L and venous pH above 7.3. The decision to transition to a subcutaneous insulin regimen also takes account of whether the patient can eat and drink. Follow the local diabetes pathway for safe IV-to-subcutaneous overlap. Source: https://abcd.care/sites/default/files/site_uploads/JBDS_Guidelines_Current/JBDS_02_Single_page_pathway_March_2023.pdf International consensus: https://doi.org/10.1007/s00125-024-06183-8","high_yield_points":["Biochemical triad: Hyperglycaemia, Ketonemia, Metabolic Acidosis.","Start IV fluids according to the adult DKA pathway and reassess volume status, including cardiac or renal limitations.","Individualise potassium replacement using serial levels, renal function, urine output and the adult JBDS pathway.","Use fixed-rate insulin in the standard adult pathway. Check potassium first and obtain urgent review for hypokalaemia.","Continue long-acting basal insulin (Lantus/Levemir).","Adult JBDS biochemical resolution requires blood ketones <0.6 mmol/L and venous pH >7.3. Bicarbonate and oral intake are not additional biochemical criteria."],"pitfalls":["Stopping long-acting (basal) insulin during FRIII.","Failure to recognize euglycaemic DKA (associated with SGLT-2 inhibitors).","Inadequate potassium replacement leading to life-threatening arrhythmias."]}}]$batch$::jsonb) ORDER BY value->>'id' LOOP
  SELECT jsonb_build_object('model_answer',m.model_answer,'high_yield_points',m.high_yield_points,'pitfalls',m.pitfalls)
    INTO STRICT v FROM public.viva_model_answers m WHERE id=(r->>'id')::uuid AND question=r->>'question' FOR UPDATE;
  IF v = r->'after' THEN CONTINUE; END IF;
  IF v IS DISTINCT FROM r->'before' THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unexpected existing viva content for %', r->>'id'; END IF;
  UPDATE public.viva_model_answers SET model_answer=r->'after'->>'model_answer', high_yield_points=r->'after'->'high_yield_points', pitfalls=r->'after'->'pitfalls' WHERE id=(r->>'id')::uuid;
  GET DIAGNOSTICS updated_count = ROW_COUNT;
  IF updated_count<>1 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unexpected row count'; END IF;
 END LOOP;
END $operation$;